1. Your headline is too generic
High priority
“Websites and ads that sell” is memorable, but it misrepresents the business. You also sell software, CRM implementation, AI automation, corporate branding, tax services and offline advertising.

Worse, “sell” is a promise. Where is the evidence that your websites and ads actually generate sales?

Fix: Position the company around business growth, then use a specific subheading to explain how you deliver it.

2. There is almost no proof
High priority
I see “13 services,” “Fast turnaround,” and “100% Direct execution.” I don't see named clients, case studies, results, testimonials, portfolio examples or a reason to believe you can execute this much work.

For a company asking businesses to trust it with their money, technology and marketing, that is a serious weakness.

Fix: Show three strong case studies with the client's problem, your work, the timeline and measurable results. Use real numbers, not invented claims.

3. Thirteen services look like a catalogue, not a strategy
High priority
The service grid gives almost equal weight to everything. Paid ads, CAC registration, video production and custom software appear to be equally central to your business.

A prospective client can't tell what you're particularly good at, what to hire you for first, or how the services fit together.

Fix: Group the services into four understandable business outcomes, then organise the individual capabilities beneath them. Keep the complete list, but stop presenting it as 13 unrelated businesses.

A. Marketing & Customer Acquisition
Get attention. Generate leads. Win customers.
- Paid advertising: Meta, Google and TikTok
- Social media management
- Influencer marketing
- SEO and Local SEO
- Content creation and video production
- Local and outdoor advertising, including billboards












B. Digital Products & Technology
Build the digital infrastructure your business needs.
- Websites and e-commerce
- Mobile apps and custom software
- Website maintenance and updates
- CRM and marketing technology deployment
- Integrations, workflows and AI automation












C. Brand & Business Intelligence
Present a credible brand and make better decisions.
- Corporate branding and brand identity
- Market and competitor research
- Customer and market insights












D. Business Setup & Compliance
Help businesses formalise and maintain their operations.
- CAC business registration
- Tax filing and compliance support
- Related administrative services you can reliably deliver

4. The site doesn't explain why one company should handle all this
Trust problem
A business owner may wonder whether your team has experienced developers, media buyers, videographers, tax professionals and automation specialists—or whether you're outsourcing everything.

Neither model is inherently bad. The problem is leaving the client to guess.

Fix: Explain your delivery model, show the people or specialist partners involved where appropriate, and identify the person responsible for each engagement. Don't claim an in-house team if you don't have one.

5. The design itself needs work

The hero wastes too much space.

The headline and buttons occupy a small area, and then there's a large stretch of empty space before “What we do.” Minimalism is fine, but this doesn't use the space to build confidence or move visitors toward an enquiry.

Add a featured project, a client result, or a clear illustration of your delivery process. Keep the clean aesthetic; make the space earn its place.




The typography is distinctive but overused.

The decorative serif works well for headlines, but repeating it across the service names makes the page feel more editorial than commercially persuasive. Some of the supporting text is also quite small.

Keep the display typeface for major headings. Use a clean, highly legible sans-serif for service titles, descriptions, navigation and body copy.




The service grid is too text-dependent.

Thirteen cards with tiny icons and one-line descriptions are easy to scan, but they don't communicate much value. The icons are decorative rather than informative.

Give the four main service groups more visual weight. Within each group, use concise descriptions, deliverables and links to detailed service pages. You don't need 13 enormous cards or 13 separate landing pages if demand doesn't justify them.




The bottom of the page doesn't close the sale.

“Ready to grow?” is a reasonable closing headline, but it repeats the same generic invitation rather than resolving the visitor's remaining doubts. The footer is also thin on useful company information.

Add a final, specific invitation, a short explanation of what happens after contacting you, your service area, business details and accessible Privacy Policy and Terms links.

6. One important distinction: Real estate video production could sit under Marketing & Customer Acquisition as a specialised offering, rather than being presented as a separate core service. Give it its own landing page if real estate is an important revenue source.

Similarly, CRM and AI automation should not be buried as one small card. If you can implement actual business workflows, connect systems and deploy useful automation, that is a potentially valuable service line in its own right.

Business registration and tax filing are different. They're often compliance-driven, relatively transactional purchases, whereas software and marketing can support larger engagements and recurring revenue. Keep them on the site if they generate profitable business, but don't let them define the main brand.

